.TH GIT-@-SWEEP 1 "August 2025" "GitAT v1.1.0" "Git Workflow Management Tool"
.SH NAME
git-@-sweep \- Clean up local branches (merged + remote-deleted)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git @ sweep
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsweep\fR command cleans up local branches by removing merged branches and branches that have been deleted on the remote. It helps maintain a clean repository by removing obsolete branches.
.PP
The command is safe by default, with confirmation prompts for destructive operations and protection for trunk branches.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run
Preview what would be deleted without actually deleting
.TP
.B --force
Force deletion without confirmation prompts
.TP
.B --merged
Only delete branches that have been merged
.TP
.B --remote-deleted
Only delete branches that no longer exist on remote
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show help message
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Clean up branches (with confirmation):
.RS
.nf
git @ sweep
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview what would be deleted:
.RS
.nf
git @ sweep --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Force clean without confirmation:
.RS
.nf
git @ sweep --force
.fi
.RE
.SH SAFETY FEATURES
.PP
The sweep command includes several safety features:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Protected branches (master, main, develop, trunk) are never deleted
.IP \(bu 2
Confirmation prompts for destructive operations
.IP \(bu 2
Dry-run mode to preview changes
.IP \(bu 2
Validation that branches are actually merged or remote-deleted
.RE
.SH WORKFLOW
.PP
1. Identifies merged local branches
.br
2. Identifies branches deleted on remote
.br
3. Excludes protected branches
.br
4. Presents list for confirmation (unless --force)
.br
5. Deletes confirmed branches
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
.BR git-@ (1),
.BR git-@-sprout (1)